              "text": "**Hipparchus of Nicaea** (c. 190 \u2013 c. 120 BC) was an ancient Greek astronomer, geographer, and mathematician. Often referred to as the \"father of astronomy\" and the \"founder of trigonometry,\" Hipparchus is credited with inventing several revolutionary mathematical concepts, scientific systems, and astronomical instruments. \n\n### 1. Mathematical Inventions\n* **Trigonometry and the \"Table of Chords\":** Hipparchus is widely recognized as the inventor of trigonometry. He compiled the first known **table of chords** (contained in a lost 12-book treatise), which is the historical equivalent of a modern table of sines. This allowed astronomers to solve any triangle mathematically rather than relying on tedious geometric reconstructions.\n* **360-Degree Division:** While the concept originated in Babylonian mathematics, Hipparchus was the first to systematically introduce the division of a circle into 360 degrees to the Greek world, applying it directly to astronomy and geography.\n\n### 2. Astronomical and Sighting Instruments\n* **The Planispheric Astrolabe:** Historians widely credit Hipparchus with inventing (or at least pioneering the math behind) the **astrolabe**. By utilizing **stereographic projection**\u2014a mathematical method of projecting a 3D celestial sphere onto a 2D flat plane\u2014he laid the groundwork for an analog computer that could tell the time, track stars, and aid in navigation. \n* **Hipparchus's Dioptra:** He invented (or highly refined) a specialized **dioptra** (or diopter). This sighting instrument consisted of a four-cubit-long rod with a sliding sight. By sliding the barrier until it perfectly blocked out the sun or moon, he was able to measure the exact apparent angular diameters of both celestial bodies.\n* **The Armillary Sphere and Celestial Globe:** To map the stars, Hipparchus designed and used an **armillary sphere** (a physical model of the celestial sphere made of interlocking rings). He also constructed highly detailed, physical **celestial globes** made of dark wood, using light-colored dots and coordinate lines to represent the night sky.\n\n### 3. Systemic and Observational Inventions\n* **The Stellar Magnitude Scale:** To catalog the brightness of stars, Hipparchus invented a classification system that divided stars into six categories of brightness. The brightest stars were called \"first magnitude,\" and the dimmest stars barely visible to the naked eye were \"sixth magnitude\". Modern astronomers still use a refined version of this scale today.\n* **The First Comprehensive Star Catalog:** Prompted by the sudden appearance of a new star (likely a supernova), Hipparchus compiled the Western world's first systematic star catalog, mapping the precise coordinates and magnitudes of approximately 850 stars.\n* **Latitude and Longitude Grid:** In geography, Hipparchus was the first to systematically apply a coordinate grid (latitude and longitude lines) to the Earth's surface. He also invented a method to calculate longitude by comparing local time differences during a lunar eclipse.\n\n### 4. His Greatest Discovery: Precession of the Equinoxes\nWhile not a physical device, Hipparchus\u2019s most famous intellectual breakthrough was discovering the **precession of the equinoxes**. By comparing his star observations with records from astronomers 150 years prior, he realized the positions of the stars had slightly shifted over time. He correctly deduced that this was caused by a slow wobble in the Earth\u2019s rotational axis.\n\n### 5. Connection to the Antikythera Mechanism\nModern historians have linked Hipparchus to the famous **Antikythera Mechanism**\u2014the world's oldest known geared analog computer. The device utilizes highly sophisticated, non-uniform gear ratios to model the variable, \"anomaly\" speed of the Moon's orbit. Because Hipparchus was the astronomer who discovered and mathematically formulated these lunar anomalies, many scholars believe he was either the inventor of the mechanism or the scientific mind behind its design.",
